00:01:41.187 --> 00:01:42.520
And then we're going to wrap up.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.630
Time willing, we're going
to take some questions.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:46.510
So I want to keep
it interactive,

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.160
so let's get started.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.090
So lifecycle.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:56.990
So I think it's very
important to highlight this,

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.390
that incidents are inevitable.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.340
We're all going to have them.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.090
No matter the amount of
auto healing and anything

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.220
that we put together,
it's bound to happen.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:10.970
And I think it's important to
appreciate that and recognize

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:15.410
that, because that enables us
to then plan for that case.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:16.280
What can we do?

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.390
What things are in place?

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.670
What processes do we
have to put in place

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:22.460
to make sure we can
respond appropriately?

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:24.980
And that's a key part of how
we do incident management

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.580
at Google.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:32.020
So just a quick rundown of a
very simplified, admittedly,

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.860
view of the internet lifecycle--

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:36.440
where things start and
where things end up.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:38.540
Usually, there's a
root cause event.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.290
And usually, it's-- high
percentage, 70% or something--

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:43.730
if these are based on change--

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.970
change in code,
change in config,

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:48.830
some other activity
causing a problem.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:50.750
That leads to some
kind of detection.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:53.690
So either a human detects
it or your cloud support.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:55.910
Your support team tells you.

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:58.670
Or an automated detection
system detects it.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:00.530
But then soon after,
some human, hopefully,

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.875
is aware of the problem,
and that starts a response.

00:03:03.875 --> 00:03:05.500
So usually, it's some
initial diagnosis

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.440
as to how bad the problem
is and what's going on.

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:09.880
And then the meat
of the activity

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.020
happens in this investigation.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.190
And what typically goes on there
is that you're gathering data,

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:18.070
and the human is doing some
kind of correlation process

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:19.695
to figure out what's wrong.

00:03:19.695 --> 00:03:24.770
So you can figure out the
best action to mitigate.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.370
After some sign of
successful mitigation,

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:32.630
we then sometimes fork into how
do we capture lessons learned.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:35.720
And a very traditional process.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.545
Bigger cases is something
like a postmortem.

00:03:38.545 --> 00:03:39.920
In some cases, it
can be smaller.

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:42.003
And I'll go through some
of those more lightweight

00:03:42.003 --> 00:03:46.100
processes of how that can be
helpful of captured lessons

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.150
learned.

00:03:47.150 --> 00:03:49.490
And then the resolved
state that we call it,

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:51.980
it's really about
fixing that bug.

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:53.210
So we mitigated the problem.

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:55.370
That doesn't mean we
actually fixed the bug.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.350
That could be something
as quick as a rollback.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.970
But then the actual bug
fix could take days.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:03.440
It could take involvement
with several teams.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.330
So you want to make
that a different stage.

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:07.430
But that's really kind
of outside the scope

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:09.860
of what I consider
the tooling that we're

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:12.460
going to talk about today
in incident response.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:16.130
IRM doesn't want to be a tool
that is doing code changes,

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.862
and config changes, and so on.

00:04:18.862 --> 00:04:20.570
We recognize it as
part of the lifecycle,

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:24.470
but we don't want to be
involved in actual directly

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:25.490
that activity.

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:28.820
And that's why that's a
little bit different in color.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:30.380
But it's key to
recognize that we

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.080
don't consider the incident
done until all those stages are

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.070
complete.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:36.970
And that connection of
that lifecycle is key,

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:40.280
and you can extract a lot of
interesting elements and data

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:42.030
from that to help future things.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:45.180
And that's one of the key parts
of the data management idea.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.790
So let's take it
a little further.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.720
So just to cover some
more elements here.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.230
I typically break down
incident lifecycle

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:55.430
into three major pillars.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.440
One is people management.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.960
And this, I think, many
are probably familiar with.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:05.060
This is the on-call rotation
kind of setup, the schedule.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.880
How do you get the right
alert to the right person.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:10.700
This part of the people, getting
that first kind of notification

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:14.240
to the human, that component,
that's what this covers here.

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:19.020
Now we are not doing this
because there's a lot of--

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:19.520
sorry.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:20.990
Let me rephrase that.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:22.970
We are not directly
investing in this right

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:24.890
now, because there's a
lot of great existing

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:27.200
tools in the marketplace
that cover that.

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:31.640
However, where Google
has a lot of expertise

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:35.100
from our various years
in incident response is

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:39.230
in process management,
efficient ways

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:41.660
to coordinate and command
an incident and make sure

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:44.420
there's no gaps or
overlaps in their response.

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:48.470
And this emerged from the
emergency response system

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:52.190
that came out in the '70s from
firefighting that happened here

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:53.940
in California.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:59.270
And so we adapted
that for the IT space,

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:02.330
using the similar
structure pyramid

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:05.462
of roles and responsibilities.

00:06:05.462 --> 00:06:06.920
And we'll go into
that a little bit

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:09.900
and how that fits
into the tooling.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:13.240
And the last place that
is the newer item in IC,

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.130
and this is the data management.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:18.340
And this is going to elaborate
more what I mean by that.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:21.100
In today's system, especially
in Stackdriver and GCP,

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.820
there's a wealth
of data available.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:24.250
We have metrics.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:25.120
We have logs.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:26.480
We have traces.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.360
We have profile information.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.360
There's a wealth of data
available the user can, when

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:34.630
an alert fires,
possibly can look at.

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:37.570
So the question is, how
can the system itself

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:41.620
help you manage that
data, so you can maximize

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:45.870
your [INAUDIBLE] investigation?

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:47.740
If there's a path
of investigation,

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:51.550
how can we leapfrog you in data
points down that investigation?

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:54.460
We mentioned earlier,
that investigation period

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:57.640
is about gathering data
and doing that correlation.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:59.685
So how can we do some
correlation for you,

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:01.060
and then jump you
along that path

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:02.518
that you're going
to do as a human?

00:07:02.518 --> 00:07:05.470
And leave the tougher
analysis that humans

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:10.602
do so well that computers
still struggle at for you.

00:07:10.602 --> 00:07:12.310
And that's one of the
key elements of it.

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:14.800
And then data in the
other senses as well.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:19.390
So the data in terms
of, when you first

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:23.200
start to respond, how do we
make it so that we maximize

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.353
your situational
awareness, so you become

00:07:26.353 --> 00:07:28.270
aware of what the problem
is, what's the blast

00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:30.590
radius, and so on.

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:32.200
And then how do we
manage that data

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:36.860
that's what you track as
your response over time,

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:38.870
so you can be more
efficient in the future?

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:40.810
So all these pieces
of data, we're

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:43.360
talking about existing
ops data, information

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:45.790
about the system
of GCP, and data

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:47.710
that you're adding
into the system.

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:52.537
As a human, curating
that data is important.

00:07:52.537 --> 00:07:54.370
So just a quick back
and forth for a second,

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:57.030
talk about the process
management element.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:59.880
It's important to review
what Google's view of this is

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:02.880
and then how data
management slots into that.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:06.030
So first, it's Incident
Management at Google.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:07.770
You might hear this called IMAG.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:10.500
It's mentioned in
the two SRE books,

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.080
and it's really the protocol
we use inside Google

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:14.530
for incident response.

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:16.360
Now a quick mention about IRM.

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:20.430
IRM is, internally, the next
generation incident response

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:24.090
tool for Google to use in
its early response system.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:27.180
So we're replacing about at
least three tools and a couple

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:29.490
of hanger-off tools as
well, consolidating that

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:30.990
into one tool.

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:33.559
We're providing that
same tooling externally

00:08:33.559 --> 00:08:35.882
through Stackdriver
as IRM as-is.

00:08:35.882 --> 00:08:37.590
I want to get to the
demo, because that's

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:39.600
all the exciting stuff.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:42.090
But what that tool
then provides is

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:47.062
years of battle-tested processes
that cover three main things.

00:08:47.062 --> 00:08:48.270
And this is the process part.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:53.170
So one, the idea
of coordination,

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:56.230
the idea that you need to
be able to recognize roles

00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:58.405
and-- sorry, not roles.

00:08:58.405 --> 00:09:00.350
Yeah, so roles and
responsibilities.

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:02.860
So that idea I mentioned
several times of,

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:05.260
we don't want gaps or
overlaps in effort.

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:06.940
The SRE books, the
workbook especially,

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:08.980
talks about the
idea of, if someone

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:11.720
is responding to an
incident, and someone else,

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:14.032
with all good intentions,
says, oh, I can help,

00:09:14.032 --> 00:09:15.990
and they jump in and they
start doing something

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:18.320
but the other person has
no idea what's going on,

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.530
that can actually
make things worse.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:23.320
So you need to establish
this idea and practice

00:09:23.320 --> 00:09:26.850
that, as a team, we're going to
respond to incidents this way.

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:28.750
We're going to know
who's going to do what.

00:09:28.750 --> 00:09:31.240
And we know who is in
charge in terms of their--

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.745
typically, called the
incident commander.

00:09:33.745 --> 00:09:35.870
So it's important to have
these processes in place.

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:37.900
And this goes back to
the idea of saying,

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:39.760
an incident is going to happen.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:42.247
Even if you typically don't
have a long history of them,

00:09:42.247 --> 00:09:43.830
you've got to have
a process in place.

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:47.370
So when it does, you can
get back to happy customers

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:50.413
and back to sleep, possibly.

00:09:50.413 --> 00:09:51.330
I'm really into sleep.

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:53.490
You can tell.

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:55.770
Second part is communication.

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:58.080
And just from life,
we know communication

00:09:58.080 --> 00:09:59.250
is super-important.

00:09:59.250 --> 00:10:01.560
But in internet management,
it goes to that same thing.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:04.470
In that case or scenario
of someone was responding

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:07.750
and that good-intentioned
colleague wants to jump in,

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:08.617
they could jump in.

00:10:08.617 --> 00:10:10.200
They need to communicate
with someone.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:11.820
They should know to go
to the incident commander

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.140
and say, hey, I can help out.

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:14.700
I know this system.

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:16.780
And they could be granted
a role to help out.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:20.220
So it's about communication,
and it's several levels

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:21.060
of communication.

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:23.010
So one, within the team.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:25.110
At the lowest level
of the response, who's

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:27.130
doing what in the response.

00:10:27.130 --> 00:10:30.000
And then you need to
communicate outside the team,

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.730
within your organization.

00:10:32.730 --> 00:10:35.520
So many times, VPs
want to know when

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:37.420
something public is going on.

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:41.340
If your thing is on Twitter
with some bad feedback,

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:45.180
some VP starts going, hey, man,
what's going on with this issue

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.450
that I'm seeing?

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:49.650
And so you want to keep them
informed, but not just informed

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:51.540
when they ping
you-- proactively.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:53.582
You don't want them on
your back as you're trying

00:10:53.582 --> 00:10:54.780
to respond to the incident.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:57.360
And so part of the IMAG system
is actually sharpening up

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:01.290
the role of the activities of
who's responding to the problem

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.150
and who's actually
communicating,

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:05.500
and then who's actually
commanding as well.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:08.970
And so you can start to see a
separation of responsibilities

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:12.190
and how they all work
together in concert.

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:15.390
And then the third level, if
the incident tends to need it,

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:19.140
is communicating
outside the organization

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:21.365
through your support
channels, or through Twitter,

00:11:21.365 --> 00:11:23.125
or through your
status dashboard.

00:11:23.125 --> 00:11:24.750
Telling your customers
what's going on.

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:26.170
Because otherwise,
they get antsy,

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:31.270
and you erode that well-built
goodwill and trust.

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:34.450
So this is all
part of that idea.

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:38.500
And lastly, the
idea of control--

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:41.510
the sense that you have to
keep up with what's going on

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:46.127
and be mindful of the scope
and impact of the incident.

00:11:46.127 --> 00:11:48.460
And so this is bigger, a lot
of the role of the incident

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:50.947
commander to say, hey,
do we need more people?

00:11:50.947 --> 00:11:52.780
I have a com lead, and
I've got an ops lead.

00:11:52.780 --> 00:11:55.090
But, man, this thing is
really getting out of control.

00:11:55.090 --> 00:11:57.570
Do I need to get
more people involved?

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:00.210
In the protocol-- and
this works at Google,

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:03.670
but you have to work with
your team to see if it works--

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:06.880
a commander could ask your boss
to become part of the incident.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:09.220
And the commanders then
telling their boss what to do.

00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:10.880
It's not about hierarchy.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:14.290
It's about the process and
about working in that moment

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:16.210
to solve the problem.

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:19.480
So that's the idea
behind control--

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:22.570
being mindful of that and
trying to address the problem.

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:27.200
I mentioned here in the
slide about heroics.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:28.630
It shouldn't take
heroics whenever

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:32.650
an incident comes around to get
you back to happy customers.

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:33.860
That should be the exception.

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:36.910
It should be orderly that you're
not extending your workday.

00:12:36.910 --> 00:12:40.210
So that means a clean shift
handoff and an orderly process

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:40.820
with that.

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:42.460
So the commander
goes, you know what?

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:43.590
Man, it's 5 o'clock.

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:45.335
I got dinner plans
with my family.

00:12:45.335 --> 00:12:46.960
I need to find a new
incident commander

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:48.002
and hand off the process.

00:12:48.002 --> 00:12:50.502
And that should be an orderly
process, and that's part of it

00:12:50.502 --> 00:12:51.077
as well.

00:12:51.077 --> 00:12:52.660
That should be built
into the process.

00:12:56.170 --> 00:13:01.660
So I wasn't sure how good
people could see this.

00:13:01.660 --> 00:13:04.650
Can people in the back
see this all right?

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:05.160
No?

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:05.700
Not at all?

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:07.110
OK, thank you for that feedback.

00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:09.230
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:09.230 --> 00:13:10.020
I tell you what.

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:12.270
We'll look at one for the
demo, the data management.

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:16.680
I'll switch over to
another incident that

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:20.650
has some of this-- this
is kind of filled out,

00:13:20.650 --> 00:13:22.770
but I'll try to get you
the same information.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:25.410
But just by regional
areas, the things

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:28.020
I just talked about in terms
of the processes and elements

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.270
are reflected here.

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:33.017
This incident, I recreated
it from the SRE Workbook.

00:13:33.017 --> 00:13:35.100
I don't know how many of
you are familiar with it,

00:13:35.100 --> 00:13:36.870
but in Belgium,
a couple of years

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:40.260
ago, we had four lightning
strikes at a data center.

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:43.343
And some batteries,
the UPS acted funny

00:13:43.343 --> 00:13:44.760
because of four
lightning strikes.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.800
Who would have thought, right?

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:49.740
And so some disks went
weird, and some disks

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:50.910
became non-operational.

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:53.250
It required restarting
and this whole process

00:13:53.250 --> 00:13:54.360
to get things back online.

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:57.520
In the data center, so
people were being impacted.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:01.260
And so this is recapping,
capturing the roles that

00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:04.980
were involved as described in
the postmortem, information

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:07.170
that people referenced,
and then who

00:14:07.170 --> 00:14:10.397
were trying to be communicated
and informed about.

00:14:10.397 --> 00:14:12.480
So in the lower right-hand,
you see subscriptions.

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:15.720
That's the outbound
communication to stakeholders,

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:17.670
so they're aware
of what's going on

00:14:17.670 --> 00:14:19.590
before they start pinging you.

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:21.460
On the left here at
the bottom is a link.

00:14:21.460 --> 00:14:24.025
So these are links to playbooks.

00:14:24.025 --> 00:14:25.650
And what they found
out in the incident

00:14:25.650 --> 00:14:27.990
was that we have
no process of how

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:32.595
to batch restart a bunch
of VMs and disks together.

00:14:32.595 --> 00:14:33.860
We don't know how to do it.

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:35.770
[INAUDIBLE] before.

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:40.225
So there was a process of
establishing scripts and docs

00:14:40.225 --> 00:14:41.850
and trying to capture
that information.

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:44.880
There was a process that we
started, I think, one or two.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:47.460
But the scale of
the problem needed

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:49.990
a whole different
extension of that process.

00:14:49.990 --> 00:14:51.840
So new docs spun
up to capture that.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:54.990
So this is kind of a way of
tracking that information.

00:14:54.990 --> 00:14:57.900
And then the top there
is the summary capturing

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:01.170
the status, the impact,
and the next update.

00:15:01.170 --> 00:15:03.210
So if you're a stakeholder.

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:05.230
You come by the
incident and say, OK,

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:08.040
let me see what's going on
beyond what they're saying.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:11.020
You have a human-readable--
sorry, let me rephrase that.

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:14.280
You have a in plain
English readable

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:17.160
way, a summary of the problem
and not technical jargon

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:19.450
saying, I'm running
this command.

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:20.340
And this is going on.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:23.520
But I can read that there's
this problem going on

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:25.380
and so on and so forth.

00:15:25.380 --> 00:15:28.560
It's meant to be about
consuming the data, not about

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:29.500
the details.

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:34.140
There is a different section
of the app that's for that.

00:15:34.140 --> 00:15:36.010
You also see that,
in the upper right,

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:40.950
there's a sense of the
severity and also a comms room.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:44.550
So for the low-level
responders, their details,

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:47.550
they're able to actually click
in and see all the stream

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:49.050
of data in that locker room.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:51.210
For us, it was IRC.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:55.560
So it has the mechanisms for
you to quickly get more data

00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:58.320
and get a snippet of data,
and move on, and structure

00:15:58.320 --> 00:15:59.380
that information.

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:04.560
And this is about the observer
view and the incident commander

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:07.200
view of the incident and where
they would stay and manage

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:08.770
information.

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:11.280
There's other sections meant
for the operators that are

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:13.740
more detailed at a lower level.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:16.200
And the data management
part, we'll go into,

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:18.258
that shows you,
when you get started

00:16:18.258 --> 00:16:19.800
what information is
available for you

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:21.508
and how we structure
that to get started.

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:28.610
So with that, let's talk
about data management then.

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:31.970
We talked about, initially,
coordinate, communication,

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:35.900
and control, the three
Cs of the IMAG process.

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:39.710
So data management,
with our push,

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:41.560
this brings in two more Cs.

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:42.780
So now we're up to five Cs.

00:16:46.048 --> 00:16:46.840
And first in that--

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:50.850
wait, sorry, that
didn't go there, OK--

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:53.020
is context.

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:56.290
What can we do with the data
information available to us

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:58.180
to make sure that
when you first start,

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:01.060
you have the best
context to get started

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:02.910
in terms of
situational awareness

00:17:02.910 --> 00:17:05.020
and continue that as you go?

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:08.020
So not just as you start,
but maintain that information

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:10.740
in the context of the incident
to everything that we do.

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:17.550
And then I mentioned a framework
to collect human-curated data

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:18.570
as part of that process.

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:24.630
And then the fifth is--

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:28.860
I'm cheating by saying C
here-- but autocorrelation.

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:31.940
Not only are we looking for
data just to show the user,

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:34.670
but can be correlate it with
other activities in the system

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:35.830
in the environment?

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:38.840
And especially at
GCP and Stackdriver,

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:41.210
where we're also your cloud
provider and your monitoring

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:42.290
system.

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:44.900
If you have an incident
response tool on this layer,

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:46.400
there's additional
data that we have

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:48.750
that others may
not have available.

00:17:48.750 --> 00:17:52.020
And over the past several
years, for example,

00:17:52.020 --> 00:17:53.210
Google has worked on--

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:54.560
one of our sister teams--

00:17:54.560 --> 00:17:59.390
has worked together in GCP
DevOps that maps and collects

00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:02.060
relationship data between
different entities

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:04.790
and resources
throughout environments.

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:06.620
So we see communication data.

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:09.030
We can see config changes.

00:18:09.030 --> 00:18:11.150
And this data is
resource to resource

00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:14.160
and expanding over time.

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:15.760
And so that gives
us the raw data

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:18.940
to correlate and
work on identifying

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:21.190
failure patterns that we
can raise and highlight

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:23.100
to the user.

00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:26.760
We'll visualize this
technology in a demo later on.

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:29.440
But just to highlight what
that raw information is,

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:31.840
and what we're doing,
and the benefit

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:34.600
of combining that vertical
integration of cloud

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:40.480
provider monitoring system and
incident response together.

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:43.340
OK, so what does this get us?

00:18:43.340 --> 00:18:46.880
So one, I mentioned, we're
working for fewer outages.

00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:49.155
And how does that work?

00:18:49.155 --> 00:18:51.320
With an end-to-end
lifecycle, we want

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:55.460
to make sure that, as you're
finding bugs and following up

00:18:55.460 --> 00:18:59.840
action items that you're able to
connect it to, actually, what's

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:02.570
the service that keeps
triggering these things?

00:19:02.570 --> 00:19:05.540
What's the service that keeps
putting me at false positives?

00:19:05.540 --> 00:19:08.028
What's an alert pause
I need to work on?

00:19:08.028 --> 00:19:09.695
Can we start connecting
that end symptom

00:19:09.695 --> 00:19:15.320
with the causal, structural
thing that needs attention?

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:17.570
Everyone, I'm sure, in
this room has had a case,

00:19:17.570 --> 00:19:20.762
where an incident happened when
we knew we had a follow-up bug

00:19:20.762 --> 00:19:22.970
to do that would fix, that
would address the problem,

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:25.050
but we just didn't get to it.

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:26.900
So how can we highlight
those situations

00:19:26.900 --> 00:19:30.920
better, so that the riskiest
items actually gets attention

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:32.750
from management for investment?

00:19:32.750 --> 00:19:35.960
Sometimes a rearchitecture is
needed to solve the problem.

00:19:35.960 --> 00:19:39.450
So how can we show over the
past 30 days or whatever,

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:42.620
or past two quarters that
our biggest outages were

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:45.087
caused by the same service?

00:19:45.087 --> 00:19:46.920
Connecting those things
together is actually

00:19:46.920 --> 00:19:49.820
the key part of that in
data management and lead

00:19:49.820 --> 00:19:51.770
to fewer outages.

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:56.990
Second is the idea
of shorter outages.

00:19:56.990 --> 00:20:01.750
And this goes to the two key
pillars of the idea of the data

00:20:01.750 --> 00:20:03.310
management and IRM.

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:07.870
And that's the idea of context
and continuous learning,

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:12.760
that through automated ways
and through low-friction ways,

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:15.460
users are able to input
data that we can then

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:19.787
leverage for future occurrences,
so that the time to more

00:20:19.787 --> 00:20:21.370
valuable information
and understanding

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:24.910
the current situation
gets shorter and shorter.

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:29.710
Now I threw numbers out
there for appreciation,

00:20:29.710 --> 00:20:32.990
not necessarily this is
exactly what would happen.

00:20:32.990 --> 00:20:34.990
But we want to make
sure that each time

00:20:34.990 --> 00:20:37.030
that you're having
the same alert fire,

00:20:37.030 --> 00:20:38.790
and it's a similar
situation, then

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:40.630
it should take you less time.

00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:44.800
A system should help you learn.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:46.750
You don't have to be
the same responder

00:20:46.750 --> 00:20:48.510
to actually benefit
from the previous time

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:49.760
that you addressed this issue.

00:20:52.740 --> 00:20:56.300
Then there's also the
case of postmortems.

00:20:56.300 --> 00:20:58.220
So postmortems are
a very common way

00:20:58.220 --> 00:21:00.830
to capture, traditionally,
lessons learned.

00:21:00.830 --> 00:21:03.740
But I bet, if I polled this
room on how many people actually

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:06.845
did postmortems, it'd
be quite a low ratio.

00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:11.150
It's like vegetables, right?

00:21:11.150 --> 00:21:12.650
Everyone knows
they're good for you,

00:21:12.650 --> 00:21:15.080
but nobody actually eats them.

00:21:15.080 --> 00:21:16.680
No one eats them enough.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:18.860
I'll put it that way.

00:21:18.860 --> 00:21:20.630
This is my weird joke now.

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:23.120
We're trying to be the ranch
dressing for those vegetables,

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:25.040
so for postmortems.

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:32.430
How do we get you to create more
postmortems with less effort?

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:36.500
How can we take that data that
we identified and you curated,

00:21:36.500 --> 00:21:39.230
put it together, and generate
the majority of that postmortem

00:21:39.230 --> 00:21:40.460
together?

00:21:40.460 --> 00:21:44.150
So those are the things
we're working towards.

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:47.830
And then lastly,
smaller outages.

00:21:47.830 --> 00:21:49.770
So this is the example
of a correlation,

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:52.350
and we'll go through a live one.

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:54.740
This is the example of the
insight that we'll do--

00:21:54.740 --> 00:21:57.270
correlating a learning metric
with a metric in your system

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:58.850
that's related to it.

00:21:58.850 --> 00:22:03.920
And so while correlation
insights are important,

00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:05.990
most importantly,
we want to make sure

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:08.930
that we're focused on this
information at the right stage

00:22:08.930 --> 00:22:10.430
of the lifecycle.

00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:12.260
And when the incident
starts, the focus

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:15.530
there should be mitigation,
not root cause analysis.

00:22:15.530 --> 00:22:17.663
Sometimes people fall
into that trap of saying,

00:22:17.663 --> 00:22:19.580
oh, let me go find that
line of the bug that's

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:23.250
causing the problem when a
rollback could be faster.

00:22:23.250 --> 00:22:25.310
We want to make sure
that we're providing

00:22:25.310 --> 00:22:27.560
data that helps information
that targets you

00:22:27.560 --> 00:22:30.620
to mitigate the problem
and stop what we usually

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:31.920
call the trigger.

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:34.730
So the code might be the
bug, but a deployment

00:22:34.730 --> 00:22:37.550
was the trigger or the
redirection of traffic

00:22:37.550 --> 00:22:39.200
from the config was the trigger.

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:41.150
How do we identify
those events so

00:22:41.150 --> 00:22:43.895
that you can stop the impact to
your users as fast as possible?

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:50.130
And then later, once
we've mitigated,

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:52.500
recognize that event,
and then switch

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:55.110
to information that's
more focused and helpful

00:22:55.110 --> 00:22:57.780
to do the resolution.

00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:00.330
So understanding those stages
of the lifecycle elements,

00:23:00.330 --> 00:23:03.990
and what that flow looks like,
and point your information data

00:23:03.990 --> 00:23:07.620
focused to each of
those at the time.

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:09.078
So with that,
let's go do a demo.

00:23:09.078 --> 00:23:11.370
I'm going to have to do a
little bit of gymnastics here

00:23:11.370 --> 00:23:14.320
to get to the right thing,
but we should be all right.

00:23:14.320 --> 00:23:17.957
So before I started the
talk, I did something

00:23:17.957 --> 00:23:18.790
at the Hipster Shop.

00:23:18.790 --> 00:23:20.320
Anybody familiar
with Hipster Shop?

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:25.330
It's the most commonly
broken app ever.

00:23:25.330 --> 00:23:27.650
But we ticked off something
that caused a problem.

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:29.440
And so here in
our Slack channel,

00:23:29.440 --> 00:23:31.630
we now get an alert
saying something

00:23:31.630 --> 00:23:33.050
happened to Hipster Shop.

00:23:33.050 --> 00:23:35.800
So let's take a look what would
happen in terms of what you

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:39.430
get in IRM based on that alert.

00:23:39.430 --> 00:23:42.120
So this is a live demo, so
may be a little bit janky.

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:45.430
I mentioned,
Stackdriver is an alpha.

00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:48.060
So if you see any
bruises or short things,

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:50.190
it's because it's alpha,
but available to sign up.

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:52.460
Let's talk about it afterwards.

00:23:52.460 --> 00:23:55.620
So here, in terms of
context, what do we provide?

00:23:55.620 --> 00:23:57.330
So we know it's slow firing.

00:23:57.330 --> 00:23:58.940
We have a chart
of the volumetric.

00:23:58.940 --> 00:24:00.960
Immediately we see
that, yep, it went up

00:24:00.960 --> 00:24:03.400
around the time of the alert.

00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:05.370
It's still up.

00:24:05.370 --> 00:24:07.860
What's going on there?

00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:11.820
We can then see that we have
an insight that triggered.

00:24:11.820 --> 00:24:15.420
When the latency
went up, the CPU

00:24:15.420 --> 00:24:18.830
shot up for this pod
contained at the same time

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:21.450
or right before,
and then sustained.

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:22.710
So it could be related.

00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:26.370
So we're not promising that
we're identifying root cause

00:24:26.370 --> 00:24:29.973
directly, but we're trying to
jump you down or leapfrog you

00:24:29.973 --> 00:24:31.890
down that investigation
path that you're going

00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:34.030
to follow naturally anyway.

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:38.750
So this is key information
that you could use immediately.

00:24:38.750 --> 00:24:42.430
Now there's also links here to
tools, like metric explorer.

00:24:42.430 --> 00:24:44.230
And metric explorer
gives you a way

00:24:44.230 --> 00:24:46.300
to kind of slice and dice data.

00:24:46.300 --> 00:24:49.840
And this link is a link
that will convey the context

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:51.960
of the incident into that tool.

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:55.602
So we would preload the metric
of the correlated metric

00:24:55.602 --> 00:24:57.310
to the time of the
incident, and then you

00:24:57.310 --> 00:24:58.440
can start adjusting things.

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:03.490
So one useful scenario
here is that, if you have,

00:25:03.490 --> 00:25:09.450
let's say, a fleet of VMs or
GKE clusters in different zones

00:25:09.450 --> 00:25:12.280
and you have an alert
happening, you could then

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:14.290
use metric explorer to
quickly slice and dice

00:25:14.290 --> 00:25:16.000
your data to see
if it's centered

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:18.880
to one zone or one region.

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:21.850
Maybe it's one region,
and you can just drain it.

00:25:21.850 --> 00:25:25.070
So it's about
conveying the problem

00:25:25.070 --> 00:25:26.530
that you're having
into the tools

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:29.440
that you use for your ops
navigation rather than you,

00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:32.020
as a human, starting
from scratch each time.

00:25:32.020 --> 00:25:34.990
Same thing applies here
to these log links.

00:25:34.990 --> 00:25:37.480
So this is a link to
logs that would reduce

00:25:37.480 --> 00:25:39.850
the time of the logs
for the last 30 minutes

00:25:39.850 --> 00:25:43.030
around the time of the incident
based on the resource it is.

00:25:43.030 --> 00:25:46.450
And for log-based metrics,
we then also apply the query.

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:49.420
So we are filtering it down
to exactly what was related

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:52.330
to populating and triggering
that incident-- or that alert,

00:25:52.330 --> 00:25:53.960
sorry.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:56.730
And same thing,
metric explorer here,

00:25:56.730 --> 00:26:00.980
we would jump to the chart of
the alerting metric populated

00:26:00.980 --> 00:26:03.190
with that information.

00:26:03.190 --> 00:26:05.290
So that's the data
in a situation

00:26:05.290 --> 00:26:06.910
that we have to
provide immediately

00:26:06.910 --> 00:26:08.560
upon arriving on the page.

00:26:08.560 --> 00:26:12.220
So that's a typical improvement.

00:26:12.220 --> 00:26:13.840
We then go further down.

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:16.220
This is a duplicate of
the metric correlation,

00:26:16.220 --> 00:26:18.580
so just ignore that.

00:26:18.580 --> 00:26:20.763
We then go down and you
see that hey, actually,

00:26:20.763 --> 00:26:22.180
there's some similar
incident that

00:26:22.180 --> 00:26:25.567
happened similar to this
alert that's firing right now.

00:26:25.567 --> 00:26:27.400
And we can see the
information that's there.

00:26:27.400 --> 00:26:29.530
We see cause tags that--

00:26:29.530 --> 00:26:32.110
you guys can't-- can
you guys read that?

00:26:32.110 --> 00:26:32.910
I'll read it out.

00:26:32.910 --> 00:26:34.870
So there's cause tags on each--

00:26:34.870 --> 00:26:38.500
cause bug, cause experiment,
and cause config.

00:26:38.500 --> 00:26:40.890
So three different
causes that happened.

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:41.570
Ignore the time.

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:44.740
This is because of me preparing,
so it all happened yesterday.

00:26:44.740 --> 00:26:47.366
This is an unrealistic
map, that sense.

00:26:47.366 --> 00:26:50.740
But the actions
were all different.

00:26:50.740 --> 00:26:51.250
Sorry.

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:52.360
The actions were all the same.

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:54.700
So three different causes, but
the actions were all the same.

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:56.783
So that gives you an
indicator that, for this kind

00:26:56.783 --> 00:26:58.810
of alert, typically, you
can solve the problem

00:26:58.810 --> 00:27:00.040
with a failover.

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:02.020
So just upon
arriving on the page,

00:27:02.020 --> 00:27:03.970
based on what people
did the last time,

00:27:03.970 --> 00:27:06.250
I don't have to go find
the postmortem in some doc

00:27:06.250 --> 00:27:09.640
somewhere or some email
and figure it out.

00:27:09.640 --> 00:27:11.500
I wasn't the person
on call, but I

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:13.750
have this history and
knowledge now baked

00:27:13.750 --> 00:27:16.690
into the tool at my fingertips
in the context of the incident

00:27:16.690 --> 00:27:18.410
that's happening right now.

00:27:18.410 --> 00:27:20.620
So that's what we're aiming for.

00:27:20.620 --> 00:27:22.120
In this particular
case, I could see

00:27:22.120 --> 00:27:24.310
that, also, the first
two are resolved,

00:27:24.310 --> 00:27:26.450
and the other one
is only mitigated.

00:27:26.450 --> 00:27:28.870
So maybe there's something
that is still going on there,

00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:30.380
or someone's still
working on it.

00:27:30.380 --> 00:27:33.550
But we know that users
are no longer impacted.

00:27:33.550 --> 00:27:34.660
So it's just a context.

00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:39.180
And we also see the severity
of these examples as well.

00:27:39.180 --> 00:27:42.650
So for this case, I'm going
to then start investigating.

00:27:42.650 --> 00:27:49.920
So I see that, given
the CPU correlation,

00:27:49.920 --> 00:27:52.036
this is the usual--

00:27:52.036 --> 00:27:53.530
this is a little
unusual, because I

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:55.870
have to write 10 feet
apart from my eyes.

00:28:02.800 --> 00:28:03.790
All right.

00:28:09.250 --> 00:28:11.853
So that's just part
of the key core data

00:28:11.853 --> 00:28:14.020
when you get started in
front of the data management

00:28:14.020 --> 00:28:16.030
idea that's possible--

00:28:16.030 --> 00:28:19.630
leveraging data from different
systems and also the ability

00:28:19.630 --> 00:28:21.610
to framework data out.

00:28:21.610 --> 00:28:26.500
I mentioned this idea of
leveraging the same framework

00:28:26.500 --> 00:28:28.220
for human-curated data.

00:28:28.220 --> 00:28:32.110
So in this section here of
the investigation update,

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:33.970
right now, it's
very heavily taxed.

00:28:33.970 --> 00:28:35.680
But the next thing
we're going to do here

00:28:35.680 --> 00:28:39.050
is capture the actual ops
data you want to look at.

00:28:39.050 --> 00:28:40.570
So instead of saying
and noting here

00:28:40.570 --> 00:28:43.990
that I looked at chart
blah-blah-blah metric

00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:46.240
in container
blah-blah-blah, instead,

00:28:46.240 --> 00:28:50.450
through Stackdriver dashboard,
you push the chart to IRM.

00:28:50.450 --> 00:28:54.040
And so this would be a
screenshot presented here

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:55.090
of the chart used.

00:28:55.090 --> 00:28:57.730
But not only that, we would
capture the metadata of that

00:28:57.730 --> 00:28:59.050
chart you looked at.

00:28:59.050 --> 00:29:01.510
So in that previous
page, not only then

00:29:01.510 --> 00:29:02.700
we have the data for you.

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:03.880
We would show you the chart.

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:06.213
If you always went to that
chart when this kind of alert

00:29:06.213 --> 00:29:08.080
happened, we would just
show you the chart.

00:29:08.080 --> 00:29:10.420
So this is the kind of
way you can leverage

00:29:10.420 --> 00:29:13.210
data for the actual
incident response process

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:16.780
throughout very common
production cases.

00:29:16.780 --> 00:29:19.600
I wanted to go
through the overview.

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:23.162
Can you guys see that better
now than the picture before?

00:29:23.162 --> 00:29:25.260
OK, yeah?

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:26.240
OK.

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:27.550
So let's say I was--

00:29:30.490 --> 00:29:31.910
let me do best practice.

00:29:31.910 --> 00:29:35.463
Best practice is when you start
responding to the severity.

00:29:35.463 --> 00:29:36.380
I think this is minor.

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:38.590
I don't really know but--

00:29:38.590 --> 00:29:40.080
and this is a couple
of information

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:43.920
here about the process
elements and tools of IRM.

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:46.380
So here's a case, where
we're providing descriptions

00:29:46.380 --> 00:29:48.600
of severities to
help the consistency

00:29:48.600 --> 00:29:51.355
across different team members.

00:29:51.355 --> 00:29:52.980
So everyone is trying
to be as close as

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:56.100
possible to the same thing,
describing the same situation

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:57.733
Let's say this was a minor.

00:29:57.733 --> 00:29:58.900
You can always update later.

00:29:58.900 --> 00:30:00.660
So don't be shy about
setting the severity

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:04.240
and updating it
later is my rule.

00:30:04.240 --> 00:30:06.370
I'll set comms to
something in Slack.

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:11.143
Something.

00:30:18.050 --> 00:30:21.475
And then, let's say that
during my investigation,

00:30:21.475 --> 00:30:23.850
I realized that, actually,
this is bigger than I thought.

00:30:23.850 --> 00:30:26.910
This is actually
quite substantial.

00:30:26.910 --> 00:30:30.810
This is where you want to
engage that protocol of, oh, I

00:30:30.810 --> 00:30:31.815
need coordination.

00:30:31.815 --> 00:30:33.450
I need an actual
incident commander.

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:35.200
So right now, I'm just
a single responder.

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:39.330
It's just me who is
the nearest right now.

00:30:39.330 --> 00:30:42.570
But let's get some
more people involved.

00:30:42.570 --> 00:30:44.370
What I want to do
to kick that off

00:30:44.370 --> 00:30:46.210
is I want to escalate
the incident.

00:30:46.210 --> 00:30:50.430
So right now, this is a local
incident in my local team.

00:30:50.430 --> 00:30:52.525
But this idea of
escalating, have

00:30:52.525 --> 00:30:55.290
you guys heard of OMG,
that tool called OMG?

00:30:55.290 --> 00:30:55.950
Yeah?

00:30:55.950 --> 00:30:58.170
So escalating an
incident is kind

00:30:58.170 --> 00:31:01.680
of the parallel of getting to
an OMG level incident, where

00:31:01.680 --> 00:31:04.230
everyone in the company
can go to one place

00:31:04.230 --> 00:31:06.240
and see it and see
what's going on.

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:09.660
And with that process is
this notion of a preset.

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:12.890
So I mentioned the idea of
incidents are inevitable,

00:31:12.890 --> 00:31:14.500
and you want to plan for them.

00:31:14.500 --> 00:31:17.820
So here's a case where product
catalog service is just

00:31:17.820 --> 00:31:18.870
a weird thing.

00:31:18.870 --> 00:31:22.530
But if it gets impacted,
it slows down our shoppers.

00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:24.190
It's a known thing.

00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:26.670
So we prepared a preset
just for product log server,

00:31:26.670 --> 00:31:27.553
product catalog.

00:31:27.553 --> 00:31:29.220
You might do it on
different dimensions.

00:31:29.220 --> 00:31:32.497
But for this example, we're
just going to do it that way.

00:31:32.497 --> 00:31:34.080
And so I'm going to
apply that preset.

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:35.997
So you see that the
current severity is minor,

00:31:35.997 --> 00:31:38.460
and there's very little
information in other fields.

00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:41.155
But by setting that alone,
and then hitting Escalate--

00:31:41.155 --> 00:31:42.860
so it's a couple of clicks--

00:31:42.860 --> 00:31:46.040
I populated it with the
playbooks and the links.

00:31:46.040 --> 00:31:49.245
I identified
subscriptions for VPs

00:31:49.245 --> 00:31:51.120
and others that are
typical stakeholders that

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:52.980
want to know what's going on
when product log service goes

00:31:52.980 --> 00:31:53.610
down.

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:55.170
Because typically, it's low.

00:31:55.170 --> 00:31:57.462
We know that its
impact is on quality,

00:31:57.462 --> 00:31:59.670
because our users will start
thinking that, hey, this

00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:00.870
is weird.

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:02.460
And that's a very
lightweight way

00:32:02.460 --> 00:32:07.890
to give lessons learned,
through the structure of tags.

00:32:07.890 --> 00:32:10.260
The most common ones
are cause and action.

00:32:10.260 --> 00:32:13.200
You saw that on a previous page.

00:32:13.200 --> 00:32:16.740
But here, we action the tag of
impact just to kind of quickly

00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:17.690
capture that.

00:32:17.690 --> 00:32:20.040
And we've added some
templating to the summary now.

00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:21.920
So now, we want to
know we want to capture

00:32:21.920 --> 00:32:25.590
the current status in terms
of, is it user visible?

00:32:25.590 --> 00:32:28.053
Or is it degraded service?

00:32:28.053 --> 00:32:29.220
But these are just examples.

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:31.710
You just want to capture a
template that everyone can do.

00:32:31.710 --> 00:32:34.122
So depending on your
preset or your team,

00:32:34.122 --> 00:32:36.330
you might have particular
formation you want to know,

00:32:36.330 --> 00:32:40.240
like how many users, or
which regions, and so on.

00:32:40.240 --> 00:32:42.510
So this is an easy way
to kind of structure

00:32:42.510 --> 00:32:44.970
a summary, like we
mentioned, to be

00:32:44.970 --> 00:32:48.690
very plain English information.

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:50.680
And then a key
information we want to do,

00:32:50.680 --> 00:32:53.310
which, at this stage,
is just a plain text,

00:32:53.310 --> 00:32:56.340
but will be a structured
information to be a countdown.

00:32:56.340 --> 00:32:57.600
When is the next update?

00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:00.340
How old is the thing
you just saved to me.

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:03.220
When will I get an update
on what's going on?

00:33:03.220 --> 00:33:05.600
Typically, for
some that's major--

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:07.167
so we're just up to major now--

00:33:07.167 --> 00:33:09.000
it could be, like, 30
minutes or 20 minutes.

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:13.510
If it's huge, you want to go
to 15 minutes or something.

00:33:13.510 --> 00:33:20.690
So that's one way to quickly
turn up the gas on a response.

00:33:20.690 --> 00:33:22.220
And then I would
go in and say, OK,

00:33:22.220 --> 00:33:25.970
let's add some
people to this role.

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:27.860
I want to get an
incident commander.

00:33:27.860 --> 00:33:29.770
I don't know if my
colleague is here.

00:33:36.170 --> 00:33:38.460
And then he could go on,
and then add in a comms lead

00:33:38.460 --> 00:33:38.960
and so on.

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:40.670
But I'm the primary responder.

00:33:40.670 --> 00:33:42.780
At this stage, I would
change to the ops lead

00:33:42.780 --> 00:33:44.660
and continue to
debug the process.

00:33:44.660 --> 00:33:49.980
Team would come in and then
start adding a comms lead,

00:33:49.980 --> 00:33:51.560
perhaps an external
communications

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:53.240
lead to make sure
we're communicating

00:33:53.240 --> 00:33:54.860
with the external
world, and start

00:33:54.860 --> 00:33:57.380
slotting into those
processes and roles

00:33:57.380 --> 00:33:58.870
of the different elements.

00:33:58.870 --> 00:33:59.370
OK?

00:34:02.090 --> 00:34:06.890
So that was the heart of
the demo I wanted to give.

00:34:06.890 --> 00:34:09.620
So one thing to kind
of nail home the point

00:34:09.620 --> 00:34:12.730
of what IRM's vision is,
and that's really about,

00:34:12.730 --> 00:34:17.360
I mentioned, the fewer, shorter,
and smaller outages and working

00:34:17.360 --> 00:34:20.120
towards that, leveraging
data management.

00:34:20.120 --> 00:34:25.280
So there's lots of
potential in this approach,

00:34:25.280 --> 00:34:27.260
but there's a great
foundation in place.

00:34:27.260 --> 00:34:29.750
We plan to build on
top of that in IRM

00:34:29.750 --> 00:34:35.075
to help our users reduce their
median time to mitigation.

00:34:39.170 --> 00:34:42.190
And love your feedback.

00:34:42.190 --> 00:34:48.780
And so in the Cloud
Next app, please rate

00:34:48.780 --> 00:34:49.904
and provide feedback.

00:34:49.904 --> 00:34:52.230
It'd be great, so the next
talk I do will be better.

00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:58.410
So with that, I'm
going to call it.

00:34:58.410 --> 00:35:01.140
Thank you, guys, so much for
coming out and hanging around.

00:35:01.140 --> 00:35:01.723
Appreciate it.

00:35:01.723 --> 00:35:02.340
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:02.340 --> 00:35:05.090
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

